\section{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
\paragraph{What an evaluator can take from this.}
A few runs give most of the information, and the choice of benchmarks matters at $k\le3$ and hardly beyond $k\approx10$. How many runs reach 90\% depends on the matrix (one on HELM MMLU, nine or more on MTEB). Models that are new in family, later or stronger than the history are predicted worse, so a $k$ chosen on random splits is too small. The closed-form risk gives the \emph{shape} of the error-versus-$k$ curve, not its level on real data.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(1) The results are for dense pools that we selected; the models outside the MTEB pool are larger, newer and stronger, and the missing-cell experiments do not go below 21\% observed cells, use the identity transform and test only models with at least 30 observed cells; missingness that concentrates on weaker models defeats selection in the history sweep. (2) The MTEB models are embedding models and the MMLU subjects are subtasks of one benchmark, so nothing here is a statement about the full set of language-model benchmarks; the dense blocks of generative-model results we assembled have $N<12$ (Appendix~\ref{app:small}). (3) The conclusions on $k^*$ are fragile on both MTEB pools (wide intervals, censoring, dependence on predictor, level and transform), and the headline $k^*$, a minimum over two predictors, is biased low by an amount the intervals bound. (4) Closure is relative to LOBO, not to the noise floor, and families are defined from model names (organisation-level families give the same conclusions, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}). (5) On real data the risk formula is off by an offset we attribute to misspecification and have not modelled; the coherence argument does not explain our data, and we have no proof for greedy selection. (6) Intervals are over families within one pool and do not cover the choice of the pool or of its benchmarks.
